@jmachowinski 

I was able to hack together a fix for now, by not removing reentrant callback groups with at least one ready entity from FIFO scheduler:

```c++
    if (ready_cbg->get_type() == CallbackGroupType::MutuallyExclusive || !ready_cbg->has_ready_entities()) {
      ready_callback_groups.pop_front();
    }
```

Here's the execution trace after this change:

<img width="2734" height="1634" alt="Image" src="https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/a180f2db-35de-4fe6-a716-ca889ce7149c" />

I have to dig deeper to make sure that this does not affect the scheduler in a bad way. I will create a PR when I'm more certain.

@jmachowinski 

Hello, I made some progress on this. Here's the summary of what I have changed:

I added a `type` field to `CallbackGroupHandle` that reflects the type of the underlying callback group.

To make sure that reentrant callback groups remain in the `ready_cbg_queue` of the scheduler, I added a condition to push them back into the queue:
```c++
CBGScheduler::ExecutableEntityWithInfo FirstInFirstOutScheduler::get_next_ready_entity_intern()
{
  while(!ready_callback_groups.empty()) {
    // ...
    if (ready_cbg->get_type() == CallbackGroupType::Reentrant && ready_cbg->has_ready_entities()) {
      ready_callback_groups.push_back(ready_cbg);
    }
    // ...
  }
}
```
I'm pushing the group again rather than preventing its popping, cause I'm afraid that a reentrant callback group that always has ready entities will cause starvation for other groups. I have made similar  changes to `get_next_ready_entity_intern(max_id)`.

I changed the `callback_group_ready()` function to check whether the group was already in the queue or not:
```c++
void callback_group_ready(CallbackGroupHandle *handle, bool callback_group_was_idle)
{
  {
    std::lock_guard l(ready_callback_groups_mutex);

    // Reentrant callback groups might not be removed from the queue when one of
    // their entities starts executing.
    if (handle->get_type() == CallbackGroupType::Reentrant) {
      bool already_in_queue = false;

      for (auto it = ready_callback_groups.begin(); it != ready_callback_groups.end(); it++) {
        if (*it == handle) {
          already_in_queue = true;
          break;
        }
      }

      if (!already_in_queue) {
        ready_callback_groups.push_back(handle);
      }
    } else {
      ready_callback_groups.push_back(handle);
    }
  }

  if(callback_group_was_idle) {
    unblock_one_worker_thread();
  }
}
``` 

Here's the current behavior of the executor:

<img width="2734" height="1634" alt="Image" src="https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/ceb279d2-cc05-407a-a860-c866e11117f8" />

Do these changes look alright to you?

